# Rich text

What renders a turn's text richly, and what a markup language would have to do
it. The premise to check is this: Verso can syntax-highlight turns text. It can,
under two conditions that decide whether it is usable here at all. Below, what
Verso is and everything else it does, what Djot is, and where each would attach.

## What there is to render

A turn is a tree, and it is not a string. `core/turns.zig` is the whole of it: a
`Node` is `text`, or an `element` — a tag, an optional `name` attribute, and
children — and printing the tree gives the raw turn the API sees. Two
consequences fix any renderer:

- *A turn's markup is already tags.* Structure arrives as elements, so a
  rich-text layer consumes a tagged tree; it does not parse prose into one.
- *Text is a derivation.* `research/turns.dj` holds this: the tree is storage,
  the raw text is output. A display rendering is a second derivation from the
  same tree, not a change to the first.

The surface is rows. `ui/row.zig` cuts a turn's text into rows of at most
`width` cells, styling carried inside them as SGR escapes; `ui/theme.zig` names
the styles. So a renderer here ends at rows, not HTML or TeX, and its last step
is always *markup → SGR → row*.

## Verso

Verso is a Lean framework for writing *about Lean*, primarily inspired by
Scribble and Sphinx (github.com/leanprover/verso). A Verso document is a Lean
file: the markup is an alternative concrete syntax for Lean, parsed to
`Lean.Syntax`, elaborated to a data structure, compiled, then run to emit
output. The official manual is verso.lean-lang.org/doc/latest/.

: markup language
  A simplified Markdown variant: a well-nested header tree, paragraphs, three
  list kinds, description lists, quotations, code blocks with a name and
  arguments, `:::` directives, `{}` commands, `%%%` metadata, and inlines for
  `_` emphasis, `*` strong, code, links, images, `$`/`$$` math, and `{role}`
  invocations. Parsing fails fast and locally rather than applying Markdown
  precedence rules; unmatched delimiters are errors, not literals.

: genres
  A genre fixes a document's global structure, its cross-cutting state, its
  additions to the markup, and how it renders. Named in the docs: the manual
  (books such as TPIL, FPL, MIL), website, and blog genres, plus the Lean
  language reference. Every genre shares the markup; combining incompatible
  genre features is a Lean type error.

: extension points
  Roles, directives, code blocks and commands, each resolved by name to a Lean
  *expander*. New syntax is a Lean function defined in the document and used in
  the same document — Verso's markup is extensible in its host language, the
  Scribble/Sphinx move.

: elaboration and rendering
  One process from author to reader: parse to `Lean.Syntax`, elaborate, compile,
  run a traversal pass that resolves cross-references, then render. HTML is a
  typed DSL (`text`, `tag`, `seq`); math becomes `<code class="math">`.

: cross-reference database
  In genres that support it, an experimental cross-document mechanism: a site
  emits `xref.json`, another site adds it as a *remote* in `verso-sources.json`
  and runs `lake exe verso sync`, then links by semantic name — "the
  documentation for `Monad`" — rather than by URL.

: Lean code highlighting
  The one capability the premise names. It highlights *Lean*, and the README's
  reason is the reason a regexp cannot: "Lean's parser is extensible," so only
  the parser knows the tokens. Output is HTML with hovers, toggleable proof
  states and hyperlinks; each token category (constants, keywords, local
  bindings) and each message severity is a CSS variable. Proof-state display
  follows Alectryon. The CSS and JS (`highlightingStyle`, `highlightingJs`,
  Tippy, Popper, `marked`) ship as string constants, and hover data is
  deduplicated into `-verso-docs.json`.

: SubVerso (github.com/leanprover/subverso)
  Highlights Lean from any version back to 4.0.0, so a document can contrast
  Lean versions independent of its own toolchain.

: literate programming and search
  `lake query :literateHtml` renders a Lean package as a site — search box,
  module navigation, light and dark themes — configured by `literate.toml`. A
  development server (`serve`) previews the HTML locally. A full-text-search
  library builds on Lean with no additional build-time dependencies.

### What the premise needs

Verso can highlight turns text only if both of these hold:

- *The code is Lean.* The highlighter is Lean's own, and the whole project is
  framed as writing about Lean. A turn's Zig, Python or shell code is not what
  it knows how to tokenize.
- *The output is HTML.* The highlighting is HTML plus bundled JS and CSS,
  styled through CSS variables. Verso has no terminal genre; its outputs are
  HTML and TeX. Producing rows means writing a new backend over the same token
  annotations, with Lean still in the loop.

And the price is the Lean toolchain: a document is compiled to an executable, so
using Verso from Zig means driving a Lean build. run1 is Zig and its display is
rows, so for a piece of a terminal draw this is a very large dependency.

[INFERENCE] the per-span token categories Verso computes are exactly what a
terminal highlighter wants; only the *emission* is HTML-shaped. The value is in
the annotations, not the backend.

## Djot

Djot (djot.net) is John MacFarlane's light markup: it keeps CommonMark's
features, simplifies the parser, and adds more — definition lists, footnotes,
tables, insert/delete, highlight, super/subscript, math, smart punctuation,
attributes on any element, and generic containers.

- *Blocks*: paragraphs, `#` headings, `>` block quotes, bullet/ordered/task and
  `:` definition lists, ` ``` ` code blocks, `=FORMAT` raw blocks, `:::` divs,
  pipe tables, `---` thematic breaks.
- *Inlines*: `_em_`, `*strong*`, `{=mark=}`, `{+insert+}`, `{-delete-}`, `^sup^`,
  `~sub~`, `[span]{.class}`, `` `code` ``, `` `raw`{=html} ``, links, images,
  autolinks, footnote references, symbols, and math — a verbatim span prefixed
  with `$` (inline) or `$$` (display). The dollars delimit; the backticks are
  verbatim content, so `$` may appear inside.
- *Attributes*: `{ #id .class key=val %comment }`, attached to a block by the
  line before it or to an inline by following it.
- *Divs*: `:::` opens a generic block container, nested divs using longer
  fences. In the fork here, the word after the fence names the element tag —
  `::: tag` becomes `<tag>…</tag>` — which is what makes a div the same shape as
  a turn's `element`.
- *Parsing*: strictly line-by-line, blocks before inlines, with the spec fixing
  nesting limits and requiring renderers to sanitize untrusted input.

Implementations exist in JavaScript (the reference, `@djot/djot`), Lua, Rust
(`jotdown`), Go, PHP and Prolog. Pandoc ≥ 3.1 reads and writes djot (`-f djot`,
`-t djot`; `-t native` is a parse with no rendering). Djot is not frozen: minor
syntax changes are expected.

Djot does not highlight code. A ` ```lang ` fence carries a language and the
renderer decides what to do with it, so Djot supplies the markup — paragraphs,
emphasis, attributes, generic containers, math — and nothing about tokenizing.

## Where each would attach

Both markups are trees over text, and both have a generic named container —
`:::` in Djot, `:::` in Verso — which is the one construct that maps onto
`Node.element` without loss. Neither renders rows by itself.

- Djot, as the markup: already the format of every file under `research/`, with
  a spec, a reference implementation, test suites, and Pandoc as a parse check.
  A turn's element tags become div names and its attributes become `{...}`. It
  renders the model's prose; it does not highlight.
- Highlighting, as its own step: a tokenizer for the few languages a turn
  carries, walked by `ui/row.zig` and painted from `ui/theme.zig`. Small,
  testable, no Lean.
- Verso, only when the code is Lean and the destination is HTML: exact Lean
  highlighting with proof states and hovers, at the cost of a Lean toolchain
  and a web backend. The right tool for a Verso site; the wrong one for the TUI.

## Open

- Which languages turn text actually carries, and how often — that sets how
  much a hand-written highlighter must cover.
- Whether rendering reads the abstract turn (elements intact, tags usable as
  structure) or the raw turn (tags as text, to be parsed back out).
- Whether run1 ever emits HTML. If it does, Verso's cross-reference and
  Lean-rendering machinery become relevant, and Djot with Pandoc is the lighter
  road to a static site.